course: language
lesson: 31
title: How babies learn to talk
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Babies start learning language before they can say a single word: newborns already prefer their mother's voice and the sounds of the language they heard before they were born.
explain: At about six months Baby Leo says "ba-ba-ba" and "da-da-da" and his dad cries "He said Dad!"; really Leo is practising sounds, like a musician playing scales, and real first words usually come around the first birthday.
explain: Toddlers don't just copy: when Kai says "I runned and I falled", he is using the rule "add -ed for the past", only on verbs that break the rule, which shows he has worked the rule out by himself.
explain: Children's mistakes are clues to how clever they are; calling every four-legged animal "dog" or mixing two languages in one sentence are normal steps, and they sort themselves out.
fact: Babies start learning language before birth; newborns prefer their mother's voice and the sounds of the language they heard before they were born.
fact: Babbling, repeating sounds like "ba-ba" and "da-da", usually starts around six months; it is practice, not real words yet.
fact: First real words usually come around a child's first birthday.
fact: "Ma" and "pa" are among the easiest sounds for babies, so many languages use them for parents (English "mama", Ukrainian "мама", Mandarin "māma", French "maman"); this does not prove all languages are one family.
fact: Around age 2, children put two or three key words together, like "Me want biscuit!", leaving out small words; this is called telegraphic speech and is normal.
fact: When children use a rule on words that don't follow it, like "runned" and "falled", it is called overregularisation; it shows they are learning rules.
fact: In 1958 the scientist Jean Berko Gleason made the "wug test": children who had never heard "wug" still said "two wugs", which showed they know rules, not just copied words.
fact: Children growing up with two languages often mix them in one sentence ("Я хочу biscuit!"); this is called code-switching or code-mixing and is normal, not confusion.
fact: The high, sing-song voice adults use with babies is called parentese; research shows babies pay more attention to it, and it can help them learn.
fact: Young children usually pick up accents and sounds more easily, but adults can still learn languages well and are often quicker at grammar and new words at first.
fact: Using one word for too many things, like calling a horse "dog", is called overextension; it is normal, and children learn the differences over time.
exercise: Baby Ava stares at her mum's face as she talks. Can babies learn language before they talk? | Yes, they are learning from before birth | No, they learn nothing at all until they say their first word | Only after they can walk | Only from television
exercise: What do newborn babies prefer to hear? | Their mother's voice | A stranger's voice | Total silence | Loud music
exercise: Baby Leo says "da-da-da" at six months. What is he doing? | Babbling to practise sounds | Saying "Dad" on purpose to call his father | Asking for food | Copying a dog
exercise: When do first real words usually come? | Around the first birthday | At birth | At six months | At age four
exercise: Why do so many languages have words like "mama" for mother? | "Ma" is one of the easiest sounds for babies | All languages on Earth come from one single first word, "mama" | English spread the word everywhere | Mothers chose the word together
exercise: Tom's sister, 2, says "Me want biscuit!" What is this called? | Telegraphic speech | Babbling | Code-switching | Overextension
exercise: Toddler Kai says "I runned and I falled." What does this show? | He has learned the rule for the past and uses it too widely | He can't hear properly | He is copying bad grammar he hears from the adults around him | He will never learn to speak well
exercise: What is the best way to help Kai? | Say the right form naturally: "Oh, you ran and fell?" | Make him repeat the right words ten times before he can play again | Tell him off every time | Ignore him until he gets it right
exercise: In the wug test, a child says "two wugs" about a made-up creature. What does it show? | Children know rules, not just copied words | Children have heard of wugs before in a picture book | Children only copy words they hear | Children like drawings
exercise: Who made the wug test in 1958? | Jean Berko Gleason | Noah Webster | Scott Fahlman | William Caxton
exercise: Mia's little cousin says "Я хочу biscuit!" What's going on? | Normal code-mixing in a child with two languages | She is confused and her family should switch to one language only | She has forgotten Ukrainian | She has forgotten English
exercise: Ella's mum talks to her baby in a high sing-song voice. What is it called? | Parentese | Telegraphic speech | Baby babbling | Nursery rhyming
exercise: Josh's dad is learning Spanish at 40. What's true? | Adults can still learn languages well | Adults can never learn new languages | Only children under 5 can learn languages | Adults can only learn to read, not speak
exercise: Sam's baby brother calls a horse "Dog!" What is this called? | Overextension | Overregularisation | Code-switching | Telegraphic speech
exercise: What should Sam say to help his baby brother? | That's a horse! | No, that's wrong, bad! | Nothing, just laugh | Dogs and horses are the same
test: Your mate goes "babies don't learn nothing till they talk, bruv." What's true? | They start learning from before birth | He's right, they learn nothing until their first word | They only learn after age three | They only learn from books
test: Grandma worries because Kai says "I goed to the park". What would you tell her? | It's a normal sign he's learned the past-tense rule | He has a serious problem and needs to see a doctor soon | He should stop talking | He has picked up a foreign accent
test: What would happen if a child only ever copied words they heard, and never worked out rules? | They could never say "wugs" about a new creature | They would speak perfectly at once, without a single mistake | They would only speak in rules | They would talk earlier
test: A Gen Z auntie says "baby talk is cringe and bad for babies, fr." ("Fr" means for real, "cringe" means embarrassing.) What does research say? | Babies pay more attention to sing-song talk, and it can help | She's right, it harms babies and slows down their talking | It makes babies stop talking | It has no effect at all
test: Your uncle says "all languages come from one word mama, that proves it." What's the better explanation? | "Ma" is an easy baby sound, so many languages chose it | He's right, it proves all languages are one family | Mama is an English word that every other language copied | Mama was invented in 1950
test: Your cousin from Dublin says "the wee one's mixin' Irish and English, she's all mixed up, the craic." ("Wee" means little, "the craic" here means it's fun.) What's true? | Mixing languages is a normal step for children with two languages | She is badly confused | She should stop one language | She will never learn either language
test: In a cartoon a baby says "Good morning, Father, please pass the toast" on day one. What's wrong? | Real babies babble first and say first words around one year | Nothing, healthy babies can talk in full sentences from birth | Babies only say "toast" first | Babies speak only French
test: Leo's dad is sure "ba-ba-ba" means "bottle". What's more likely? | Leo is babbling to practise sounds | Leo really asked for his bottle | Leo is saying "baby" | Leo is singing a song
test: Your dad says "too late for me to learn Ukrainian, I'm 40." What's the encouraging truth? | Adults can learn languages well, it's never too late | He's right, learning a language is impossible after the age of 12 | Only children can learn Ukrainian | Adults can only learn sign language
test: A toddler says "Me want more juice!" and her big sis says she "talks like a robot, she's thick." What's true? | Short key-word sentences are a normal stage around age 2 | She is behind other children and needs special help | She is copying robots she has seen on TV cartoons | She will always speak like that
test: Your baby cousin calls every round thing "ball", even the Moon. What should you do? | Name it for her: "That's the Moon!" | Tell her off for being so silly about it | Agree the Moon is a ball to play with | Never mention the Moon to her ever again
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises babbling in infants? | It constitutes the practice of speech sounds preceding the production of words | It constitutes the production of fully formed words | It is an indication of a disorder of hearing | It occurs only in infants of bilingual parents
exam: Identify the principal finding of the "wug test" devised by Jean Berko Gleason in 1958. | Children apply grammatical rules to words they have never previously heard | Children reproduce only those words which they have previously heard from adults | Children cannot form plurals before the age of ten | Children prefer drawings to words
exam: In respect of a child who says "I runned", select the response consistent with the established facts. | The child is applying a general rule to an irregular verb, a normal stage of development | The child is suffering from a disorder of speech requiring immediate professional treatment | The child has acquired the form from adult speech | The child should be prevented from speaking until corrected
exam: Ascertain the term designating the mixing of two languages within a single utterance by a bilingual child. | Code-switching | Overregularisation | Telegraphic speech | Bilingual babbling
